:{"position":[-10.367434150559887,2.6814006179128858,-129.51912979006698],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"araucaria-tree","options":{"height":48,"seed":243}},{"id":"9601737d-edff-4f86-96f6-132ec7f66f3c","createdAt":1786942187170,"transform":{"position":[37.82658645265142,-2.41323197892918,-140.2830706080776],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"araucaria-tree","options":{"height":36,"seed":256}},{"id":"7f4b8fb0-74d2-4a8b-b0ea-6ee7f2069c9f","createdAt":1786942187171,"transform":{"position":[66.41611777841231,-0.44677431241920784,-98.03061476237741],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"araucaria-tree","options":{"height":40,"seed":269}},{"id":"9bf3da77-2e73-429f-871f-0c05e48031c3","createdAt":1786942187172,"transform":{"position":[106.1387102260257,0.28431287409174866,-73.98726812013379],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"araucaria-tree","options":{"height":44,"seed":282}},{"id":"a44f2a24-2dc1-4526-826e-78763be0942e","createdAt":1786942187174,"transform":{"position":[95.58782553434312,0.43639826398776793,-26.886372129051338],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"araucaria-tree","options":{"height":48,"seed":295}},{"id":"8448d839-426e-4f7c-881f-fb3bd921b233","createdAt":1786942187175,"transform":{"position":[-11,-8.215650382226158e-15,37],"rotation":[0,0.3,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":-11,"z":37,"rotY":0.3,"eyebrow":"Where you are","title":"The last day of the dinosaurs","body":"Everything here lived about 66 million years ago, right at the end of the Cretaceous. Every animal on this island really did share that time and place — most famous dinosaurs never met each other."}},{"id":"ecbbd77b-05d7-4008-b277-3da71db010bb","createdAt":1786942187176,"transform":{"position":[11,-8.215650382226158e-15,37],"rotation":[0,-0.3,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":11,"z":37,"rotY":-0.3,"eyebrow":"Deep time","title":"Stegosaurus was already ancient","body":"More time separates Stegosaurus from T. rex than separates T. rex from you. The Age of Dinosaurs lasted so long that its own history had ancient history."}},{"id":"3464c406-835c-4d29-ad03-e718594cfa9a","createdAt":1786942187177,"transform":{"position":[-10,-3.774758283725532e-15,17],"rotation":[0,0.4,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":-10,"z":17,"rotY":0.4,"eyebrow":"Underfoot","title":"No grass anywhere","body":"Grass had barely appeared. The green here is ferns, horsetails and moss — which is why almost every plant-eater on this island is built to browse low shrubs, not to graze a lawn."}},{"id":"459d6fc8-bb23-49df-9160-6f60f3cee90f","createdAt":1786942187178,"transform":{"position":[10,-3.774758283725532e-15,17],"rotation":[0,-0.4,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":10,"z":17,"rotY":-0.4,"eyebrow":"Something new","title":"The first flowers","body":"Flowering plants were a recent invention in the Cretaceous. The magnolias here are among the oldest kinds still alive — T. rex lived alongside the very first blossom."}},{"id":"d5623c64-368c-4164-852b-7f0eecfd0bb9","createdAt":1786942187180,"transform":{"position":[15,-4.218847493575595e-15,19],"rotation":[0,-0.5,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":15,"z":19,"rotY":-0.5,"eyebrow":"The dig","title":"How anyone knows any of this","body":"The string grid is not decoration. Every bone is drawn and measured inside its own square before it is lifted, because where a bone sat tells you as much as the bone does."}},{"id":"f909d873-cb4d-4961-ac9a-709641271b6d","createdAt":1786942187181,"transform":{"position":[39,-0.0004227370890805826,27],"rotation":[0,-2.6,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":39,"z":27,"rotY":-2.6,"eyebrow":"The dig","title":"How a fossil forms","body":"An animal has to be buried fast — under river mud or sand — before it rots or is eaten. Minerals then seep in over millions of years. Almost nothing that dies ever becomes a fossil."}},{"id":"40ea5683-f455-4d4a-9460-f0198f0c9529","createdAt":1786942187182,"transform":{"position":[24,3.552713678800501e-15,-16],"rotation":[0,-1.5,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":24,"z":-16,"rotY":-1.5,"eyebrow":"The dig","title":"Plaster jackets","body":"Those white bundles are bones wrapped in plaster and burlap, exactly like a cast on a broken arm. It is the only way to move something that has been shattered for 66 million years."}},{"id":"0de4f564-4ce4-4934-997b-ad459b0acbe2","createdAt":1786942187183,"transform":{"position":[-21,-4.440892098500626e-16,2],"rotation":[0,1.4,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":-21,"z":2,"rotY":1.4,"eyebrow":"Triceratops","title":"Three horns and a shield","body":"The frill is bone, and the brow horns are over three feet long. They were probably used against rivals as much as against predators — plenty of frills carry healed wounds from other Triceratops."}},{"id":"951ba4b1-bce2-47f9-8a4b-481ea0167296","createdAt":1786942187184,"transform":{"position":[-18,3.1086244689504383e-15,-14],"rotation":[0,2.2,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":-18,"z":-14,"rotY":2.2,"eyebrow":"Ankylosaurus","title":"A living tank","body":"Armour plates set into the skin, spikes along both flanks, and a solid bone club on the tail heavy enough to break a leg. Even its eyelids were armoured."}},{"id":"a0860c53-09c7-4b79-9708-c5c1a437aa99","createdAt":1786942187186,"transform":{"position":[-46,-0.02314646147616939,-18],"rotation":[0,1.1,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":-46,"z":-18,"rotY":1.1,"eyebrow":"Edmontosaurus","title":"What T. rex ate","body":"Some Edmontosaurus fossils carry T. rex bite marks that HEALED. That is the strongest evidence we have that T. rex attacked living prey — and that sometimes the prey got away."}},{"id":"950d5cdb-fe8f-4e69-8a37-5daa564a8674","createdAt":1786942187187,"transform":{"position":[9,3.1086244689504383e-15,-14],"rotation":[0,-1.2,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":9,"z":-14,"rotY":-1.2,"eyebrow":"Pachycephalosaurus","title":"Ten inches of solid skull","body":"Stand next to this one — it is about your height. The dome on its head is bone up to ten inches thick, probably for shoving contests with rivals rather than head-on charges."}},{"id":"ad0dff9b-11e4-4358-887c-a5a45eef72da","createdAt":1786942187188,"transform":{"position":[25,6.8833827526759706e-15,-31],"rotation":[0,-1,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":25,"z":-31,"rotY":-1,"eyebrow":"Footprints","title":"What tracks tell you","body":"A skeleton tells you how an animal was built. A trackway tells you what it did — how fast it walked, whether it travelled alone, and how it placed its feet."}},{"id":"3845d940-4add-4434-a92c-7f6f64e3fcfc","createdAt":1786942187189,"transform":{"position":[-21,-0.010479682493685916,-43],"rotation":[0,0.5,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":-21,"z":-43,"rotY":0.5,"eyebrow":"The nest","title":"Dinosaur parents","body":"Dinosaurs built nests, sat on their eggs and looked after what hatched. That behaviour is one of the strongest links between them and the birds outside your window."}},{"id":"9c045153-9dc6-49fc-80be-dfef1b2a824d","createdAt":1786942187190,"transform":{"position":[25,-0.6158072115222735,-65],"rotation":[0,-2.3,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":25,"z":-65,"rotY":-2.3,"eyebrow":"Tyrannosaurus rex","title":"Thirteen feet at the hip","body":"Nearly three times your height at the hip, and forty feet nose to tail. The biggest teeth found are about the size of a banana — and built like railway spikes, for crushing bone rather than slicing."}},{"id":"81b319da-f7d6-494e-b797-1a1916f1dea6","createdAt":1786942187192,"transform":{"position":[-13,-0.560242708696586,-58],"rotation":[0,1.1,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":-13,"z":-58,"rotY":1.1,"eyebrow":"Tyrannosaurus rex","title":"Those arms are not a joke","body":"They are shorter than yours, but each one could curl around 400 pounds. Nobody is certain what they were for — holding struggling prey and pushing up off the ground are the best guesses."}},{"id":"2ed32ba6-7499-4382-a552-d8cc7e75f5a5","createdAt":1786942187193,"transform":{"position":[33,-0.1482357364988014,-47],"rotation":[0,-1.9,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":33,"z":-47,"rotY":-1.9,"eyebrow":"Look up","title":"That is not a dinosaur","body":"Quetzalcoatlus is a pterosaur — a flying cousin, not a dinosaur. Its wings are skin stretched on one enormous finger. Standing on the ground it was as tall as a giraffe."}},{"id":"07141443-2fc7-4c10-9e61-178e7264dde4","createdAt":1786942187194,"transform":{"position":[-36,-4.440892098500626e-15,20],"rotation":[0,0.9,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":-36,"z":20,"rotY":0.9,"eyebrow":"Living fossil","title":"You can go and touch one","body":"The ginkgo here is near-identical to the ginkgo growing in car parks today. Whatever wiped out the dinosaurs, this tree walked straight through it unchanged."}},{"id":"0e4423b1-70b2-4651-b7e0-5680501973d3","createdAt":1786942187195,"transform":{"position":[-16,-8.881784197001252e-16,4],"rotation":[0,0.7,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":-16,"z":4,"rotY":0.7,"eyebrow":"They are still here","title":"Birds are dinosaurs","body":"Not \"descended from\" — they ARE dinosaurs, the one branch that survived. Every sparrow is a closer relative of T. rex than T. rex was of Triceratops."}},{"id":"7d39c8ab-20c7-4985-814e-5090c4287f00","createdAt":1786942187196,"transform":{"position":[-39,-0.037952094134006575,34],"rotation":[0,0.8,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":-39,"z":34,"rotY":0.8,"eyebrow":"What happens next","title":"The worst day on Earth","body":"An asteroid about six miles across strikes near what is now Mexico. Within hours the sky is on fire; within years the cold and dark end three quarters of all species. Everything you can see from here goes — except the birds."}},{"id":"7118cae7-fa11-4a64-8de6-7ff4b7fbced0","createdAt":1786942187198,"transform":{"position":[-13,5.991681875921475,-45],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"light-orb","color":"#f2a541"},{"id":"e3794ce7-6b65-4c33-b44d-5b9ab1711e70","createdAt":1786942187199,"transform":{"position":[10,8.000000000000009,-40],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"light-orb","color":"#f2a541"},{"id":"56c06d4f-e5cb-4661-b30b-5c7205393e4e","createdAt":1786942187200,"transform":{"position":[-2,7.883533919513991,-52],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"light-orb","color":"#f2a541"},{"id":"e14a70d5-e7d3-44c7-a33d-e890fc20ec70","createdAt":1786942187201,"transform":{"position":[-30,7.000000000000002,-10],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"light-orb","color":"#f5f5f5"}]}